[139.798329, "o", "   660\t\r\n   661\tRisk assessment WP-2610 ties FX revaluation lag to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n   662\t\r\n   663\tLead reviewer M. Chen noted that FX revaluation lag created reconciliation noise in treasury during June rates. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n   664\t\r\n   665\tIn June rates, external advisors reviewed FX revaluation lag and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n   666\t\r\n   667\tSite visit #731 to treasury captured mailbox exports showing how FX revaluation lag correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n   668\t\r\n   669\tMemo WP-5321 summarizes stakeholder interviews about FX revaluat"]
[139.798487, "o", "ion lag. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n   670\t\r\n   671\tDuring June rates, M. Chen circulated a draft finding on FX revaluation lag. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n   672\t\r\n   673\tFollow-up #624 confirmed that treasury had been using an informal spreadsheet for FX revaluation lag. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n   674\t\r\n   675\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n   676\t\r\n   677\tIn June rates, external advisors reviewed FX revaluation lag and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative c"]
[139.798678, "o", "ontext so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n   678\t\r\n   679\tSite visit #331 to treasury captured mailbox exports showing how FX revaluation lag correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n   680\t\r\n   681\tThe committee packet references FX revaluation lag as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n   682\t\r\n   683\tThe committee packet references FX revaluation lag as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n   684\t\r\n   685\t## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence\r\n   686\t\r\n   687\tController memo (binding reconciliation rule):\r\n   688\t\r\n   689\tThe corrections desk ships multiple notices per field. When several notices\r\n   690\ttarget the same t"]
[139.798697, "o", "ransaction and field, the notice with the lexicographically\r\n   691\tgreatest `effective` date wins. For each winning notice, `previous_value` in\r\n   692\t`reconciliation_report.jsonl` is the field value immediately before that notice\r\n   693\tapplies (after ledger, amendments, and emails). Owner corrections override\r\n   694\tboth ledger owners and signed meeting amendments when applied in this step.\r\n   695\t\r\n   696\t## Investigation Brief 04 — Correction Notice Precedence — field notes\r\n   697\t\r\n   698\tSite visit #195 to corporate accounting captured mailbox exports showing how intercompany netting dispute correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n   699\t\r\n   700\tDuring April settlement, R. Okonkwo circulated a draft finding on intercompany netting dispute. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n   701\t\r\n   702\tDuring"]
[139.798711, "o", " April settlement, S. Patel circulated a draft finding on intercompany netting dispute. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n   703\t\r\n   704\tDuring April settlement, S. Patel circulated a draft finding on intercompany netting dispute. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n   705\t\r\n   706\tInterview #629 with corporate accounting highlighted how intercompany netting dispute statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n   707\t\r\n   708\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n   709\t\r\n   710\tLead reviewer L. Bergstro"]
[139.798723, "o", "m noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n   711\t\r\n   712\tIn April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n   713\t\r\n   714\tIn April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n   715\t\r\n   716\tInterview #197 with corporate accounting highlighted how intercompany netting dispute statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps"]
[139.79874, "o", ".\r\n   717\t\r\n   718\tThe committee packet references intercompany netting dispute as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n   719\t\r\n   720\tLead reviewer S. Patel noted that intercompany netting dispute created reconciliation noise in corporate accounting during April settlement. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n   721\t\r\n   722\tIn April settlement, external advisors reviewed intercompany netting dispute and asked for a machine-readable reconciliation trace. The archive preserves narrative context so auditors can explain why a given transaction received compliance_hold.\r\n   723\t\r\n   724\tDraft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n   725\t\r\n"]
