[579.214985, "o", "The committee packet references capital project capitalization as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n"]
[579.215002, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.21502, "o", "Follow-up #775 confirmed that fixed assets had been using an informal spreadsheet for capital project capitalization. The formal extractor must instead derive rows only from the canonical ledger and structured sections at file end.\r\n"]
[579.215038, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215056, "o", "Interview #913 with fixed assets highlighted how capital project capitalization statements were forwarded without the matching ledger row. Counsel advised preserving the full thread because downstream exception coding depends on mail timestamps.\r\n"]
[579.215074, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215091, "o", "Risk assessment WP-6250 ties capital project capitalization to control deficiencies around mail ingestion. Teams must not treat decoy ledger headings or appendix commentary as authoritative transaction sources.\r\n"]
[579.215108, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215127, "o", "Working paper WP-1770 documents a three-way match failure tied to capital project capitalization. The team escalated when repeated extractor runs produced different status columns for the same transaction id.\r\n"]
[579.215144, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215171, "o", "Lead reviewer M. Chen noted that capital project capitalization created reconciliation noise in fixed assets during July review. Staff initially blamed tooling, but timeline reconstruction showed manual overrides arriving after batch lock.\r\n"]
[579.215188, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215206, "o", "Site visit #134 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n"]
[579.215223, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215241, "o", "Draft guidance in this paragraph is non-binding: some early tooling compared compliance mail against post-correction effective dates when flagging holds. The mid-year amendment later in this archive supersedes that draft practice.\r\n"]
[579.215258, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.21528, "o", "Memo WP-1725 summarizes stakeholder interviews about capital project capitalization. Participants disagreed on whether unsigned amendments should ever override ledger owners; Brief 05 resolves that question for the extractor.\r\n"]
[579.215298, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215317, "o", "Site visit #521 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n"]
[579.215334, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215353, "o", "The committee packet references capital project capitalization as a contributing factor to late compliance holds. Investigators cross-checked mail excerpts against amendment minutes before accepting any owner change.\r\n"]
[579.21537, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215389, "o", "During July review, S. Patel circulated a draft finding on capital project capitalization. Finance operations pushed back, arguing the issue was transient cache state; QA reproduced the drift on a cold run the next morning.\r\n"]
[579.215407, "o", "\r\n"]
[579.215425, "o", "Site visit #638 to fixed assets captured mailbox exports showing how capital project capitalization correspondence arrived between two correction batches. Reviewers flagged the gap because effective-date logic must honor notice precedence.\r\n"]
